### 🟠 ISSUE 8 — `tau_close` sign convention causes perpetual holding

**File:** `backtest.py` lines 92–95

```python
if pos_prev == 1.0 and zt_val > tau_close:   # tau_close = -0.5 by default
    exit_now = True
elif pos_prev == -1.0 and zt_val < -tau_close:  # = zt_val < 0.5
    exit_now = True
```

For a **long** position (entered when z < −1.0), you exit when z > −0.5, i.e., the spread has risen back to normal. That is correct. But the docstring says `tau_close = -0.5` meaning "the signed threshold", which is already negative, so `zt_val > -0.5` exits correctly. However, this means a long position entered at z = -1.2 exits any time z > -0.5 — **not when it crosses zero**. In mean-reversion strategies, closing at zero (or at a small positive value) captures more of the reversion. With tau_close = -0.5, you exit **very early** and miss most of the P&L.

The Clegg & Krauss (2018) convention is to exit when Z crosses 0 (not at -0.5). Recommend: `tau_close = 0.0`.

---

### 🟠 ISSUE 9 — `transaction_cost = 0.0` by default

**File:** `backtest.py` line 34

The default transaction cost is zero. With the current strategy generating many trades, the realized P&L is significantly overstated. Any "profitable" result without non-zero transaction cost is unreliable.

