## Section 5 — Regime Detection Design

The regime classifier addresses the root cause: some time windows have a reliable mean-reverting relationship, others don't. Trading in non-MR windows is the main source of losses.

### Recommended architecture: Rolling logistic classifier

**Why not LSTM/HMM first:** Both require labeled training data or EM fitting, which is slow and introduces its own overfitting risk. A logistic classifier on rolling features is fast, interpretable, and easy to validate.

**Features (all computed causally — no lookahead):**

| Feature | Window | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `spread_zscore` | 60-day rolling | Current z-score of M_t |
| `vol_ratio` | 20/60-day | Short-vol / long-vol of spread |
| `half_life_est` | rolling | Rolling OLS AR(1) of M_t → derived half-life |
| `r2_mr` | rolling | Rolling partial cointegration R²_MR |
| `beta_change` | 20-day | Rolling std of daily Δβ_t (instability signal) |
| `return_corr` | 60-day | Rolling return correlation of X1, X2 |
| `ADF_pvalue` | 60-day | ADF test p-value on rolling M_t (mean-reversion test) |

**Label construction (for supervised training):**
- Forward-looking P&L of the spread position over the next 20 days (from formation period only)
- Label = 1 if 20-day P&L > some threshold, 0 otherwise
- **This is computed on formation data ONLY — never on out-of-sample data.**

**Training protocol:**
1. Compute labels on formation data (in-sample).
2. Fit logistic regression / small MLP on formation features.
3. On out-of-sample data: run classifier forward-only. Execute Bollinger Band trade only if `P(MR-regime) > threshold` (threshold tuned on last 20% of formation data).

